ogccdb-gen: --max-lod needs an integer, got 'x'
usage:
  ogccdb-gen build <cdb> [--elevation <dem.tif|folder>]... [--imagery <image.tif|folder>]...
                   [--min-lod N] [--max-lod N] [--resampling nearest|bilinear|cubic|average]
                   [--elevation-min-lod N] [--elevation-max-lod N] [--elevation-resampling R]
                   [--imagery-min-lod N] [--imagery-max-lod N] [--imagery-resampling R]
                   [--quality 1..100] [--extent W,S,E,N] [--no-fill] [--comment TEXT]
  ogccdb-gen list <cdb> [--dataset 1|4] [--lod N]
  ogccdb-gen info <cdb> [--dataset 1|4] [--lod N]
  ogccdb-gen check <cdb> [--dataset 1|4] [--lod N]

build creates the CDB Version <cdb> (Metadata/Version.xml) if it does not exist yet,
then imports the Elevation and Imagery sources into it. Repeat --elevation or
--imagery, or name a folder (its .tif files, by name), to mosaic several inputs of
any size, resolution and CRS; where they overlap, the finer one wins (the later one
at equal resolution).
LODs: by default from LOD -10 to as fine as each input's resolution allows, set per
dataset. --min-lod, --max-lod and --resampling apply to both datasets; the
--elevation-* and --imagery-* forms set one dataset and win over them.
--extent (degrees) imports only the tiles that overlap it, e.g. one geocell:
--extent 8,47,9,48.
Tiles are written whole (CDB 1.3 Vol 1 §8.6.1.1): where no input covers part of a
tile, that part gets the dataset default value from Metadata/Defaults.xml, else
the spec's (0 m for Elevation, gray 0.5 for Imagery). --no-fill leaves such tiles
out instead, with the finer tiles under them (§7.1.2.2 Requirement 15).
list prints the tile files of <cdb>, one path per line.
info prints, per dataset component, LOD and geocell, how many of the geocell's tiles
<cdb> holds.
check reads every file under <cdb>/Tiles and prints one line per problem: a path
that is not a CDB tile path, or a tile that cannot be read (format, size, channels,
data type). It exits with 1 if it finds a problem.
